DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppnnmmaalliiaass reads a PNM image as input, and applies anti-aliasing to back‐
       ground and foreground pixels.  If the input file  is  a  PBM,  ppnnmmaalliiaass
       promotes  the  output anti-aliased image to a PGM, and prints a message
       informing the user of the change in format.


OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In addition to the options common to all programs  based  on  libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common  Options  ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), ppnnmmaalliiaass recognizes the
       following command line options:

       --bbggccoolloorr _c_o_l_o_r_b sets the background color the _c_o_l_o_r_b.

       --ffggccoolloorr _c_o_l_o_r_f sets the foreground color to _c_o_l_o_r_f.

       Pixels with these values will be anti-aliased.   By  default,  ppnnmmaalliiaass
       takes  the  background  color  to  be black, and foreground color to be
       white.

       Specify the  color  (_c_o_l_o_r)  as  described  for  the  argument  of  the
       ppnnmm__ppaarrsseeccoolloorr(()) library routine ⟨libnetpbm_image.html#colorname⟩ .

       Note that even when dealing with PGMs, background and foreground colors
       need to be specified in the fashion described above.  In this case, ppnn‐‐
       mmaalliiaass takes the background and foreground pixel values to be the value
       of the red component for the specified color.

       --bboonnllyy says to apply anti-aliasing only to the background pixels.

       --ffoonnllyy says to apply anti-aliasing only to the foreground pixels.

       --bbaalliiaass says to apply anti-aliasing to  all  pixels  surrounding  back‐
       ground pixels.

       --ffaalliiaass  says  to  apply  anti-aliasing to all pixels surrounding fore‐
       ground pixels.

       If you specify neither --bbaalliiaass  nor  --ffaalliiaass,  ppnnmmaalliiaass  applies  anti-
       aliasing only among neighboring background and foreground pixels.

       --wweeiigghhtt  _w says to use _w as the central weight for the aliasing filter.
       _w must be a real number in the range 0 < _w < 1.  The lower the value of
       _w is, the "blurrier" the output image is.  The default is w = 1/3.


